1785467587331719	skills-dev	itc:v3:AWLJk_5k8kmTJEAAAAAAQda_7XqtagAAAAEwAQ		item.create	bn-x7r	{"description":"hook.rs register_session() returns early when location() finds no pane:\n\n    let (mux, mux_session, pane) = location();\n    if pane.is_empty() { return; }\n\nlocation() knows zellij, tmux, wezterm and kitty. An agent hosted anywhere else\n-- a PTY runtime such as vessel, a bare terminal, a CI job -- exports none of\nthose variables, so nothing is written at all.\n\nThe early return is right about the location: with no pane there is nowhere to\nattribute the agent to, and inventing one would be worse. But the record also\ncarries three facts that have nothing to do with a multiplexer and are thrown\naway with it:\n\n  - pid + starttime: an exact process identity, the thing that lets any external\n    supervisor say 'this session is the agent I spawned'\n  - transcript: the only structured source for a Claude session's final answer,\n    since its turn_end carries no result\n\nVerified: a vessel-hosted Claude guest that ran and answered correctly had zero\nlines in register.jsonl.\n\nConsequence for consumers: with no pid to join on, identity has to be recovered\nfrom behaviour -- 'the session whose cwd matches and which has been active since\nI pressed Enter'. That works, and is what the vessel-agents skill does, but it\nis ambiguous the moment two agents share a working directory, which is the\nnormal case for a fleet reviewing one repo. A pid would make it exact.\n\nProposal: always append the record, with mux/mux_session/pane empty when there\nis no multiplexer. Subscribers that render a pane already have to handle an\nempty pane (it is cleared when a binding goes stale), so an empty one here\nshould need no change on their side.\n\nNow that agentbus is decoupled from zcode and explicitly multi-host, 'hosted in\nsomething we do not recognise' seems worth supporting as a first-class case\nrather than a silent drop.","kind":"bug","size":"s","title":"register_session drops pid, starttime and transcript when no multiplexer is detected"}	blake3:_5a8l_tQX6wf0uXmJqC47HvRVGIGAKmTlwaV669BPRE
1785467599283707	skills-dev	itc:v3:AWLJk_5k8kmTJEAAAAAAQda_7XqtagAAAAEwAg		item.create	bn-1vj	{"description":"claude.rs emits turn_end from the turn_duration system record, which has no\ntext, so result is hardcoded None:\n\n    out.push(Kind::TurnEnd { duration_ms: ..., result: None });\n\ncodex.rs populates result with the agent's answer. So the same event kind means\ntwo different things depending on source, and every consumer that wants 'what\ndid it say' has to special-case Claude and go read the transcript itself --\nlocating it first, which is its own problem (see the register_session bone).\n\nMeasured: a codex turn_end arrived with result 'BANANAS'; the equivalent Claude\nturn returned result null while the answer sat in its transcript.\n\nThe normaliser exists so that adding an agent means writing one normaliser\nrather than touching every subscriber. This asymmetry pushes exactly that work\nback onto subscribers.\n\nThe last assistant text block is already reachable from the transcript the\nnormaliser is tailing: keep the most recent non-empty {type: text} content\nblock seen since the last turn boundary, and attach it when turn_duration\narrives. Clamping it the way hook results are clamped would keep the line\natomically appendable.\n\nWorth deciding deliberately either way -- if result is meant to be\n'only when the agent volunteers one', that is defensible, but then it should be\ndocumented as source-dependent so consumers know to expect null.","kind":"task","size":"s","title":"Claude turn_end carries no result, while Codex's does"}	blake3:ijRBsn4lO-DVQyrYk52M4a1B9gBl0tIu8LXkvCA2ioQ
1785467612872896	skills-dev	itc:v3:AWLJk_5k8kmTJEAAAAAAQda_7XqtagAAAAEwAw		item.create	bn-1wg	{"description":"codex.rs emits Kind::Prompt; claude.rs emits none (grep: 2 vs 0). A Claude turn\nstarts only through Kind::Reported{state: working} from a hook, or is inferred\nfrom a tool call.\n\nThis is a live trap, not a theoretical one. A wait loop written as 'see a prompt\nfor this session, then wait for turn_end' works against codex and hangs forever\nagainst Claude. Measured while building the zellij-agents skill: Claude answered\nin about 1s and the loop still timed out at 120s, because the gating event never\nexisted.\n\nThe workaround is to gate on the folded snapshot state instead of on any event\nkind, which is what both skills now do and document. That works, but it means\nthe event log -- the thing advertised as tailable and replayable -- cannot by\nitself tell you when a Claude turn began, only the snapshot can. Anyone\nsubscribing to events rather than polling the snapshot will hit this.\n\nClaude's normaliser deliberately emits Label rather than Prompt for the\nlast-prompt record, and that reasoning is sound: the record is written after\nturn_duration, so treating it as a turn start pins the session to working\nforever.\n\nSo the gap is a turn-start signal that is not that record. Options: emit a\nturn-start when a user message is first seen in the transcript, or promote the\nhook-reported working state into a first-class turn_start event kind so both\nagents produce a matching pair of boundaries.\n\nEither way the goal is that turn_start/turn_end bracket a turn for every source,\nso a subscriber can follow turns without knowing which agent it is watching --\nwhich is the stated point of the normalised vocabulary.","kind":"task","size":"s","title":"No turn-start event for Claude, so waits gated on prompt hang forever"}	blake3:ZvSgihas7Nq_17dXeUhenh5UHbdv5FMqNQT5UHGPeNU
